{"title":"How plants make their own food","key_idea":"Plants use sunlight, water, and air to make sugar and oxygen in a process called photosynthesis.","method":["Plants take in water through their roots from the soil","Plants take in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny holes in leaves called stomata","Sunlight gives energy to turn water and carbon dioxide into sugar (food) and oxygen","The plant uses sugar as food to grow and stay alive"],"worked_example":{"problem":"A plant sits by a sunny window. What three things does it need to make food?","steps":["The plant needs sunlight from the window","The plant needs water from the soil (given by watering)","The plant needs carbon dioxide from the air around it"],"answer":"Sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide are the three things a plant needs to make food."},"key_terms":[{"term":"photosynthesis","meaning":"the process where plants use sunlight, water, and air to make food"},{"term":"stomata","meaning":"tiny holes in plant leaves where carbon dioxide enters"},{"term":"sugar","meaning":"the food that plants make and use to grow"}],"common_mistakes":["Thinking plants get all their food from the soil — they make most food using sunlight","Forgetting that plants need air (carbon dioxide), not just water and light"],"remember":"Sunlight + water + carbon dioxide = sugar + oxygen.","quick_check":[{"q":"Where do plants take in water from?","a":"Through their roots from the soil"},{"q":"What are the tiny holes in leaves called where air enters?","a":"Stomata"}],"tell_your_child":"Plants are clever — they use sunshine to cook water and air into food, just like a kitchen makes a meal! Without sunlight, water, and air together, plants cannot make the sugar they need to grow."}
```
